From bb96fcddb07a1ae09303a01837d0774aac9a88f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ben <60955077+tencur@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:13:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] vesuvius.predict: average sign-invariant directions as
 orientation tensors

TTA variants and overlapping patches may report an axis with either sign.
The fixed half-space 4x + 2y + z >= 0 left exact opposites on its plane,
such as (1, -2, 0) and (-1, 2, 0), opposite, and gave nearly parallel
axes on either side of it, such as (1, -1.999, 0) and (1, -2.001, 0),
opposite signs, so they cancelled where the patches overlap, in the
in-memory and in the staged blend. With TTA disabled, axes reported with
opposite signs cancelled the same way, as on main.

Axis heads (inplane_direction, and nearest_component trained with the
shipped sign-invariant loss) are now averaged as the orientation tensor
v v^T, which does not depend on the sign or order of the predictions: over
the TTA variants, and in both blenders, which accumulate the three
off-diagonal components in extra float32 sums after the output channels.
The mean tensor is reduced to its principal eigenvector (repeated
squaring, a fixed number of rows at a time), scaled to the square root of
the largest eigenvalue: predictions that agree up to sign keep their
common vector and length. The output keeps three components per axis
head, written in the half-space above as a sign convention; signed heads
and the nearest_component distance are averaged as before.

vesuvius.predict marks these heads `sign_invariant` in target_info (also in
the logits attrs) for blend_logits and blend_and_finalize; logits written
without the mark are blended as before.

